<main id="main">
<section class="ihero"><div class="ihero-text"><div class="ihero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Claim glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Glossary: Alaska Edition</h1><p class="lede">44 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, plus a few that are unique to Alaska, defined in plain English.</p></div></div><figure class="ihero-photo"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-documents.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-documents-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-documents.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 55vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></figure></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="gfilter"><label for="gq">Search 44 terms</label><input id="gq" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “snow load”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gcount></p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-G">G</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a></div><dl class="gloss"><div class="term" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Often the first payment on a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs of living elsewhere while your home is being repaired after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either side demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side appoints to value the loss. Policies often require appraisers to be competent and independent.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Arctic entry</dt><dd>An enclosed entry vestibule common in Alaska homes that limits heat loss. It is part of the dwelling for claim purposes.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-B"><dt>Barge freight</dt><dd>Shipping materials by barge to coastal or river communities, often on a seasonal schedule. A real cost in remote repairs.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Borough</dt><dd>Alaska&#x27;s equivalent of a county. The state has 19 organized boroughs plus the Unorganized Borough.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-C"><dt>Census area</dt><dd>One of 11 statistical divisions of Alaska&#x27;s Unorganized Borough, used where no borough government exists.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage for the house itself and attached structures.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage for detached garages, shops, sheds and fences.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage for contents.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of your policy listing coverages, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The part of a covered loss you pay before the policy pays.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, used to calculate actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-E"><dt>Earth movement exclusion</dt><dd>A common exclusion for earthquake, landslide, settlement and similar ground movement.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Earthquake endorsement</dt><dd>An addition to a policy that provides earthquake coverage, usually with a percentage deductible.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Estimate</dt><dd>An itemized list of the work and materials needed to repair a loss, with quantities and prices.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-G"><dt>Glacial outburst flood</dt><dd>A sudden release of water dammed by or beside a glacier, such as the floods on Juneau&#x27;s Mendenhall River.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation withheld until repairs are complete.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-I"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the eaves that traps meltwater, which can then leak into the home.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering roof membrane installed in vulnerable areas such as eaves and valleys to resist leaks.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry on an estimate, such as a quantity of drywall or roofing at a unit price.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether repaired areas must reasonably match undamaged areas, which can expand the scope.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps taken to prevent further damage, such as tarping, drying, heating or snow removal.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-N"><dt>Named peril</dt><dd>A cause of loss specifically listed as covered in a policy.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of meeting current building codes when repairing a damaged building.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor&#x27;s charges for managing a project, often expressed as percentages.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-P"><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of a coverage limit rather than a flat dollar amount.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Permafrost</dt><dd>Ground that stays frozen for two or more years. Thaw-related settlement is usually excluded from coverage.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which the insurer may request within a set time.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional who represents a policyholder in presenting and negotiating a claim.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the policy will pay once repairs or replacement are completed.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace with new materials of like kind and quality, without deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The full description of the damaged areas and items, and the work needed to repair them.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Snow load</dt><dd>The weight of accumulated snow and ice on a roof, measured in pounds per square foot.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An additional cost for working on steep roofs, typically above a certain pitch.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Suit limitation clause</dt><dd>The policy condition limiting how long you have to file a lawsuit against the insurer.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missed or underpaid in the original estimate.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Surplus lines</dt><dd>Insurance written by insurers not licensed in the state, used for risks the standard market does not cover.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Unorganized Borough</dt><dd>The large part of Alaska not within an organized borough, divided into census areas for statistics.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-W"><dt>Weight of ice, snow or sleet</dt><dd>A named peril in many homeowner policies covering damage caused by the weight of accumulated snow and ice.</dd></div></dl><p class="hint" data-gnone hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-stick"><div class="side-box call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through</h2><p>Free first conversation. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn gold block" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm Alaska Time</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Learn</h2><ul class="side-list"><li><a href="/appraisal-clause-process/">Appraisal process</a></li><li><a href="/alaska-appraisal-faq/">Appraisal FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-glossary/" aria-current="page">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/alaska-claim-guides/">Claim guides</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
